<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gilbert Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> Islands are a chain of sixteen coral atolls in the central Pacific, inhabited by Micronesian I-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over them in 1892, joined them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> as a colony, and they became the core of independent Kiribati in 1979. The atoll kingdom of Abemama lay in the central group.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands.jpg" alt="Gilbert Islands" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilbertIslandsPos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gilbert Islands, called Tungaru in Gilbertese and formerly the Kingsmill Islands, lie about halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and form the eastern-most part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micronesia">Micronesia</a>. They run in a rough north to south line, with the northernmost island, Makin, about 780 kilometres from the southernmost, Arorae. They are grouped as the Northern, Central and Southern Gilberts. In the north, a chief living on Butaritari ruled Butaritari and Makin with full authority, while in the south power was held collectively by the unimwane, the old men of the island. The north has more rainfall, which allows a wider range of crops. Tembinok', the last king of Abemama, Kuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aranuka">Aranuka</a> in the central group, died in the early part of the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands had been inhabited by Oceanians for at least 2,000 years and probably 3,000. In 1606 the Portuguese navigator Pedro Fernandes de Queirós sighted Butaritari and Makin. Vice-Admiral John Byron passed through in 1765. In 1820 Adam Johann von Krusenstern named them after Captain Thomas Gilbert, who had crossed the archipelago in 1788, and the French captain Louis Duperrey was the first to map the whole group. Whaling ships called in numbers from 1825 until the 1860s, and ships of the United States Exploring Expedition charted reefs and anchorages.</p><p>An Anglo-German agreement of 1886 placed the islands in the British sphere of influence, and Captain Edward Davis of HMS Royalist proclaimed a British protectorate on 27 May 1892. The conduct of the second resident commissioner, William Telfer Campbell, including allegations of forced labour, was the subject of Arthur Mahaffy's report of 1909. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> were later proclaimed a colony. In the early and middle 20th century islanders earned income mainly from phosphate production on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a>, and the missions ran the village schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Japanese invaded the islands on the day of the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> and occupied three of them by 9 December 1941. On 17 August 1942, 221 US Marines raided Makin; the raid is believed to have alerted the Japanese to the islands' importance and led to their fortification. US forces landed on Makin and Tarawa on 20 November 1943, and the Japanese were defeated.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> became autonomous in 1971. The Ellice Islands were separated from 1975, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuvalu">Tuvalu</a> became independent in 1978. In 1979 the Gilberts became the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>, whose name is the Gilbertese rendering of Gilberts. They make up the main part of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gilbert Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1892 Britain declared protectorates over the <b>Gilbert Islands</b>, including the Kingdom of Abemama, and the Ellice Islands, to forestall other powers and regulate the labour trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>The chiefs of Abemama in the central <b>Gilbert Islands</b> built up their power in the mid-19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abemama">Kingdom of Abemama</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Gilbert Islands</b> gained independence as the Republic of Kiribati on 12 July 1979, with the Phoenix and Line Islands and Banaba included.</blockquote><a href="/state/kiribati">Kiribati</a></li></ul></section>
